  "answer_core": "I would not choose the Silva direction yet, but I also would not default to the safer lab path. The decision should turn on two gates, not on which story feels more exciting or safer.\n\nFirst, test whether Silva can become a dissertation: usable data you can access soon, a collaborator deep enough to offset your lab's lack of single-cell experience, and explicit advisor support. A hot field plus uncertain data is not a plan. Identify specifically who would cover the single-cell work and what technical execution remains yours.\n\nSecond, protect the fallback while you test Silva. It is only real if it remains advisor-backed, resourced, technically ready, identity-coherent, and protected by a stop-loss date. Give the Silva probe a short, dated window—decide in advance what evidence makes you continue or stop, and what makes you walk away.\n\nThe sequence is advisor first, low-cost Silva reality check second, committee only after you know whether the data path is real. Do not go around your advisor with a finished proposal, do not run all options in parallel, and do not treat broad PhD success-rate claims as probabilities for this case. With a three-month deadline and an advisor retiring in 2-3 years, choose only after both executability gates have evidence.",
